<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William I of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Soldier King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William I was King in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1713 to 1740, known as the Soldier King. He cut court spending, reformed the finances and the bureaucracy through the General Directory, and roughly doubled the army to around 80,000 men, one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for the size of the population. The army and the full treasury he left allowed his son Frederick II to seize Silesia in 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick William I of Prussia" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Pesne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_Pesne_-_K%C3%B6nig_Friedrich_Wilhelm_I._von_Preu%C3%9Fen_(ca._1733).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
